      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the length of the parametric curve \\((x,y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\), we use the distance formula for parametric curves. The length \\(L\\) of the curve from \\(t = a\\) to \\(t = b\\) is given by\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2} \\, dt.\n\\]\n\nFor the given parametric equations \\(x = 2 \\sin t\\) and \\(y = 2 \\cos t\\), we first compute the derivatives:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = 2 \\cos t,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{dy}{dt} = -2 \\sin t.\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute these derivatives into the distance formula:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_0^\\pi \\sqrt{(2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2} \\, dt.\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expressions inside the square root:\n\n\\[\n(2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2 = 4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t = 4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t) = 4 \\cdot 1 = 4.\n\\]\n\nThus, the integral becomes:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_0^\\pi \\sqrt{4} \\, dt = \\int_0^\\pi 2 \\, dt.\n\\]\n\nNow, evaluate the integral:\n\n\\[\nL = 2 \\int_0^\\pi 1 \\, dt = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_0^\\pi = 2 (\\pi - 0) = 2\\pi.\n\\]\n\nThus, the length of the parametric curve is \\(\\boxed{2\\pi}\\).",
